('h2', "10.1  Introduction"),
('p', "Health care is consistently ranked among the most stressful occupations in the world. A laboratory professional works with **infectious material, expensive equipment, unforgiving deadlines, life-and-death results, night duties and demanding attendants** \u2014 often with inadequate staff and little recognition. Stress in this setting is not a personal weakness; it is an **occupational hazard** (see Chapter 8, psychosocial hazards). Unmanaged, it produces error, illness, absenteeism, burnout and the loss of good professionals from the field. **Stress management is therefore both a personal survival skill and a patient-safety measure.**"),

('h2', "10.2  Definitions and Basic Concepts"),
('def', ("Stress",
         "Stress is the **non-specific response of the body to any demand made upon it** (**Hans Selye**, 1936 \u2014 the 'Father of Stress Research'). In psychological terms, it is the state of **physical, mental and emotional tension** that arises when the **demands placed on a person exceed his perceived ability or resources to cope** with them.",
         ["The word derives from the Latin *stringere*, 'to draw tight'.",
          "**Key insight \u2014 stress is a function of *perception*.** The same night duty is exciting for one technologist and terrifying for another. Stress = (perceived demand) \u2212 (perceived ability to cope). This is why *cognitive* strategies work."])),
('def', ("Stressor",
         "Any **stimulus, event, situation, person or demand \u2014 internal or external, physical or psychological \u2014 that produces stress** in an individual. Stressors may be acute (a single needle-stick injury) or chronic (perpetual understaffing).")),
('def', ("Stress response / Strain",
         "The **physiological, psychological and behavioural reaction** of the individual to a stressor \u2014 tachycardia, sweating, anxiety, irritability, insomnia, and so on.")),
('def', ("Coping",
         "The **constantly changing cognitive and behavioural efforts** made by a person to manage specific external or internal demands that are appraised as taxing or exceeding his resources (**Lazarus & Folkman**).")),
('def', ("Adaptation",
         "The process by which an individual adjusts successfully to a stressor and restores equilibrium (**homeostasis**).")),
('def', ("Stress Management",
         "The **set of techniques, strategies and skills** used to identify stressors, to control the level of stress, and to cope with stress in a way that preserves physical health, mental well-being, professional performance and quality of life.")),

