('qa', ("QUESTION BANK \u2014 CHAPTER 10", [
    ("Define stress and describe the General Adaptation Syndrome.",
     ["Definition as in section 10.2 (Selye, 1936), noting that stress depends on **perceived** demand versus **perceived** coping ability.",
      "**GAS \u2014 three stages.** **1. Alarm reaction:** recognition of the stressor; sympatho-adrenal discharge with adrenaline and noradrenaline; HPA axis activation (CRH \u2192 ACTH \u2192 **cortisol**); tachycardia, raised blood pressure, tachypnoea, sweating, pupillary dilatation, hyperglycaemia, blood diverted to muscle, digestion inhibited; resistance initially dips then rises. **2. Stage of resistance (adaptation):** the body adapts, symptoms subside, resistance is above normal but reserves are being consumed; complete recovery is possible if the stressor is removed here. **3. Stage of exhaustion:** reserves depleted, resistance falls below normal; sustained cortisol causes immunosuppression, hypertension, hyperglycaemia and peptic ulceration; clinically fatigue, burnout, depression, infection, and the **'diseases of adaptation'** \u2014 hypertension, coronary artery disease, peptic ulcer, diabetes, asthma, autoimmune disease. Draw the three-stage flow diagram."]),
    ("Differentiate between eustress and distress.",
     ["**Eustress** \u2014 positive, motivating stress within coping capacity; improves performance, learning and satisfaction; example: preparing for an examination or taking charge of a new analyser. **Distress** \u2014 negative, excessive or prolonged stress exceeding coping capacity; impairs performance, health, judgement and relationships; example: chronic understaffing or fear of litigation. Add the **Yerkes\u2013Dodson inverted-U law** \u2014 performance peaks at an optimum level of arousal \u2014 and conclude that the aim of stress management is to **optimise, not abolish**, stress. Mention also hyperstress and hypostress."]),
    ("Enumerate the occupational stressors of a medical laboratory technologist.",
     ["Work overload and understaffing; time pressure and constant urgency; **fear of error and of litigation**; shift work, night duty and sleep deprivation; exposure to biological and chemical hazards and the anxiety following a needle-stick injury; violence and verbal abuse from attendants; role ambiguity and role conflict; **moral distress** at being pressed to release doubtful results; poor interpersonal relations, hostile supervision and bullying; lack of recognition, autonomy and career progression, with inadequate pay; equipment breakdown and resource shortage; monotony in some sections (hypostress); adverse physical environment \u2014 noise, heat, crowding, poor ergonomics, long standing; exposure to suffering and death; organisational change; and job insecurity in contractual employment."]),
    ("Describe the effects of chronic stress on the body.",
     ["Present system by system: **neuroendocrine** \u2014 sustained hypercortisolaemia and HPA dysregulation; **cardiovascular** \u2014 hypertension, coronary artery disease, arrhythmia, stroke; **respiratory** \u2014 hyperventilation, aggravated asthma; **gastrointestinal** \u2014 peptic ulcer, gastritis, irritable bowel syndrome, appetite and weight change; **metabolic** \u2014 type 2 diabetes, dyslipidaemia, central obesity; **immune** \u2014 immunosuppression with frequent infection, delayed wound healing, autoimmune flare; **musculoskeletal** \u2014 tension headache, neck and back pain, bruxism; **reproductive** \u2014 menstrual irregularity, reduced libido, subfertility; **psychological** \u2014 insomnia, anxiety, depression, impaired memory and concentration, burnout; **skin** \u2014 eczema, psoriasis, acne, alopecia. Add the behavioural consequences: increased errors, absenteeism, substance use and short-cutting of safety procedures."]),
    ("What is burnout? Describe its components, causes and prevention.",
     ["Definition (Maslach; WHO ICD-11 \u2014 an **occupational phenomenon** arising from unmanaged chronic workplace stress). **Three components:** emotional exhaustion; depersonalisation/cynicism; reduced personal accomplishment. Stages: honeymoon \u2192 onset of stress \u2192 chronic stress \u2192 burnout \u2192 habitual burnout. Causes: chronic overload, long hours and night duty, low control with high demand, lack of recognition and reward, unfairness, value conflict and moral distress, inadequate resources, and exposure to suffering. Consequences: increased medical error and poorer patient care; depression, substance misuse and illness in the professional; turnover and absenteeism for the organisation. Prevention: **individual** \u2014 boundaries, sleep, exercise, hobbies, social and peer support, mindfulness, counselling; **organisational (more important)** \u2014 adequate staffing, rational rosters, realistic workload, just culture with blame-free reporting, recognition, autonomy, clear roles, training, resources, zero tolerance of violence, occupational health and employee-assistance programmes. Distinguish from depression (pervasive) and compassion fatigue."]),
    ("Discuss the coping strategies for stress.",
     ["Classify: **problem-focused** \u2014 act on the stressor itself; the most effective approach when the stressor is controllable (request staff, repair the analyser, learn the LIS, obtain PEP). **Emotion-focused** \u2014 manage the emotional response when the stressor cannot be changed (relaxation, exercise, sharing, acceptance, prayer, humour). **Meaning/appraisal-focused** \u2014 cognitive restructuring of how the stressor is viewed. **Social-support seeking** \u2014 debriefing, peer groups, family. **Maladaptive/avoidant coping** \u2014 alcohol, tobacco, drugs, overeating, denial, aggression, absenteeism and withdrawal \u2014 which give short-term relief and long-term harm and must be identified and replaced. Conclude with the rule: **change the stressor if you can; change your response if you cannot.**"]),
    ("Describe any five relaxation techniques.",
     ["**Deep/diaphragmatic breathing** \u2014 slow abdominal breathing, e.g. the 4-7-8 pattern or paced breathing at about 6 per minute, activating the parasympathetic system. **Jacobson's progressive muscle relaxation** \u2014 systematic tensing and releasing of muscle groups from feet to face. **Meditation and mindfulness (MBSR)** \u2014 non-judgemental focused attention on the present moment. **Yoga and *pranayama*** \u2014 postures, breath regulation and *shavasana*. **Guided imagery/visualisation** \u2014 vivid imagining of a calm, safe place. Others: autogenic training, biofeedback, massage and warm baths, music and art therapy, laughter therapy, and the **5-4-3-2-1 grounding technique** for acute panic."]),
    ("What organisational measures can reduce stress among laboratory staff?",
     ["Adequate staffing and rational workload norms; well-designed forward-rotating rosters with recovery after night duty; clear job descriptions and defined scope of practice; participation in decision-making and reasonable autonomy (**Karasek's demand\u2013control model** \u2014 high demand with low control is the most damaging combination); a **blame-free, just culture** of incident reporting; recognition, fair reward and career progression; training and competency development; adequate equipment, reagents and PPE; ergonomic workplace design with rest and eating areas; zero tolerance of violence and harassment with security and a **POSH Internal Complaints Committee**; occupational health services including immunisation, PEP and periodic check-ups; confidential counselling/employee-assistance programmes; a trusted grievance mechanism; stress-management and communication training; and team-building with supportive rather than punitive supervision."]),
    ("Write short notes on Type A personality.",
     ["Described by **Friedman and Rosenman**. Features: excessive competitiveness and achievement-orientation; chronic **time urgency** and impatience; hostility, aggressiveness and irritability; rapid, emphatic speech; doing several things at once; difficulty in relaxing and guilt when idle. Significance: associated with a higher incidence of **coronary artery disease and hypertension**, hostility being the most cardiotoxic component. Contrast with **Type B** \u2014 relaxed, patient, non-competitive, lower cardiac risk \u2014 and **Type D** ('distressed') \u2014 negative affectivity plus social inhibition, also linked to adverse cardiac outcomes. Management: awareness, cognitive restructuring, relaxation training, realistic goal-setting and hostility reduction."]),
    ("How does stress affect patient safety in a laboratory?",
     ["Stress and sleep deprivation increase identification, labelling, transcription and pipetting errors; stress produces attentional narrowing or 'tunnel vision' so that flags and warning signs are missed; it impairs working memory, so verbal results are misheard and misrecorded; it erodes empathy first, producing the rudeness that generates complaints and litigation; stressed staff take short-cuts with safety procedures such as gloves, needle disposal and quality control; and **burnout is statistically associated with increased medical error**. Therefore stress management is a **core patient-safety and risk-management activity**, not a welfare luxury, and the employer has both an ethical and a legal interest in providing it."]),
])),

